End-Stage Renal Disease
Palliative & End of Life
Uremic pruritus, Oliguria/anuria, Uremic encephalopathy/asterixis

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-028
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, intractable pruritus, profound nausea, and distressing muscle cramps in a patient who has ceased dialysis.
Location: Residential Aged Care Facility
Present at Visit: Patient, registered nurse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the patient was in absolute agony from unremitting generalized scratching, resulting in excoriated, bleeding skin. The patient is also suffering from severe, painful muscle cramps and intractable vomiting. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandatory to provide immediate parenteral symptom relief for the uremic nausea and severe pruritus, and to prevent further self-mutilation and suffering prior to the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My skin is crawling and burning. I can't stop scratching, I'm throwing up everything, and my legs are cramping horribly."
History of Present Illness: The patient recently ceased hemodialysis. Over the last week, there has been progressively decreased urine output directly advancing to frank anuria. The patient suffers from profound, unremitting uremic pruritus causing severe and intractable generalized scratching. There is intractable nausea and recurrent, frequent vomiting. The nurse notes uremic encephalopathy clinically presenting as severe confusion, asterixis, and progressive lethargy, particularly in the evenings. The patient complains of an intractable metallic taste persistently reported in the mouth. There is profound generalized fatigue and weakness directly secondary to refractory renal anemia. Tonight, the patient developed severe, painful muscle cramps and visible fasciculations in the lower extremities. Progressive, generalized fluid overload completely resistant to high-dose loop diuretics is evident.
Functional Status: Bedbound; acutely distressed and continuously scratching.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of severe secondary skin infections from excoriations; severe physical distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/90 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 20 bpm | SpO2: 95% on room air | Wt: 65 Kg (estimated)
General: Patient is profoundly pale, lethargic but restless, constantly scratching arms and chest, with visible excoriations.
Physical Exam: Skin exam reveals diffuse, linear excoriations with fresh bleeding. Uremic frost is visibly manifesting as fine white urea crystals distributed on the skin surface of the face and chest. Neurological exam reveals a sluggish mentation and a distinct flapping tremor (asterixis) on wrist extension. Lower extremities show 2+ pitting edema and visible muscle fasciculations. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by the observed severe uremic pruritus (active scratching, excoriations), reported anuria by staff, and the clinically evident uremic encephalopathy/asterixis.
Environmental Observations: Emesis bags are at the bedside. The patient's fingernails are jagged and soiled with dried blood.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient is receiving baseline palliative opioids which may be exacerbating the uremic pruritus; regular antiemetics are being vomited.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: End-Stage Renal Disease - Status: Exacerbated (Severe Uremia / Palliative Phase)
Differentials:

Scabies or allergic contact dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the classic presentation of uremic frost, anuria, and asterixis directly pointing to profound uremia as the singular cause of the systemic pruritus.

Acute hypocalcemia - Likely a contributing factor to the severe muscle cramps, driven entirely by the end-stage renal failure and hyperphosphatemia.

Bowel obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely as the cause of nausea, given the profound uremia, metallic taste, and absence of colicky abdominal pain or distension.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is in the terminal phase of end-stage renal disease without dialysis support, presenting with an acute, agonizing crisis of severe uremic pruritus, nausea, and cramping. The objective clinical evidence—uremic frost, asterixis, and frank anuria—confirms profound, irreversible uremic toxicity. Because oral medications are failing due to intractable vomiting and the patient is actively mutilating their skin from the severe itch, delaying assessment until standard hours would result in hours of torturous, unmanaged suffering. Immediate after-hours medical intervention was essential to bypass the oral route with parenteral antiemetics and centrally-acting medications to blunt the neuropathic itch and muscle cramps.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered stat subcutaneous haloperidol for the centrally-driven uremic nausea. Administered stat subcutaneous midazolam to immediately break the cycle of severe muscle cramping and relieve the acute distress from the pruritus. Prescribed gabapentin (at heavily renally adjusted doses) and regular emollients for ongoing pruritus management.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you feel the intense, burning urge to scratch (trigger), firmly apply the prescribed thick, cooling emollient cream using flat palms instead of your fingernails (behavior). This soothes the nerve endings without breaking the skin, directly preventing painful bleeding and dangerous infections (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Continued high-acuity palliative nursing care within the facility.
DME/Safety (Optional): Ensure the patient's fingernails are trimmed very short and filed smooth immediately. Apply soft cotton mittens if the scratching continues during sleep.
Follow-up: Facility GP review in 24 hours to monitor symptom control and titrate subcutaneous syringe driver medications.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops severe, intractable respiratory distress, unmanageable terminal agitation, or generalized seizures, the nursing staff must administer the prescribed breakthrough subcutaneous midazolam and contact the palliative care triage line immediately.

